- The Alabama Unified Judicial System canons referenced include: Preamble (uphold integrity), Canon 2 (avoid impropriety), Canon 5 (regulate extra-judicial activities to minimize conflict), and Canon 7 (refrain from inappropriate political activity).
- Divorce and child custody cases in Baldwin County can last up to 12 years, according to the author's description.
- Presiding Judge Floyd recently implemented three administrative changes to DR/Family Court to expedite final trials: (1) answer and waiver divorces are now assigned by clerks and handled by rotating general jurisdiction circuit judges; (2) temporary motions for cases originally assigned to Judge Thomason and Judge Bosch are now heard by other four circuit judges, with Judge Floyd and Judge Bishop taking over Thomason's dockets and Judge Norton and Judge Taylor taking over Bosch's; (3) specially assigned judges will handle Judge Thomason's domestic violence dockets in 2017.
- The author notes that Judge Thomason's status quo orders typically impose immediate joint physical custody, regardless of prior family dynamics.
- The author asserts that Judge Thomason favors pro se litigants and has been described by attorneys as a venue where lawyers would pay $500 to hire an attorney rather than face a pro se opponent.

