corridor-of-chaos.compressed:
HARD FACTS & EVENTS:
- On October 28, the city council holds a public hearing to consider final approval of an application by Gayfer Village Partners for a three-part planned unit development.
- The development site is 76 acres of mostly farm land on the northwest corner of Fairhope Avenue and Hwy 181.
- Unit 1 consists of 16 commercial lots (B-2), Unit 2 consists of 232 multi-family apartment units (R-5), and Unit 3 consists of 77 single family residential lots (R-3).
- The property is currently outside city limits in un-zoned county land and will be concurrently annexed into the city upon approval, with city impact fees collected.
- Tom Mitchell is listed as the developer for the project.
- Municipal Judge Haymes Snedeker is the sole member of Gayfer Village Partners, LLC, and his business partner Ray Hix is its organizer.
- Ray Hix served on the city's Airport Authority until last year.
- Snedeker and Hix own Hix Snedeker Companies, a nationwide commercial development business.
- The property was previously owned by the Klump family, which operated an unpaved airfield known as 'Klump Pud' there.

AUTHOR'S ALLEGATION:
- The author claims that Mayor Tim Kant, Jay Robinson, and Catalyst have colluded to pass development proposals despite their stated opposition.
- The author alleges that this collusion is responsible for the cumulative impact of proposed developments at Old Battles Place (94 units), Fly Creek Apartments (240 existing + 77 planned), and the new 'Corridor of Chaos' project (566 apartments, 77 homes, and 16 commercial units).

