law-and-disorder-part-2.compressed:
HARD FACTS & EVENTS:
- On Saturday, August 25, at approximately 9:45 p.m., Fairhope police officers found numerous cars parked along the side of County Road 13, identified as creating a hazard.
- Police discovered a party at a residence in the 16000 Block of County Road 13, with an estimated 100 juveniles present; many fled on foot when officers arrived.
- Alcohol was located at the scene; approximately 40 to 50 juveniles were detained. Some had consumed alcohol, while others had not.
- Due to the number of detainees, officers identified the juveniles and contacted their parents for pickup rather than transporting them to the police department to avoid tying up all available resources.
- Lt. Hollinghead, interim chief of police, stated that the names of juveniles who consumed alcohol were documented, and officers will work with juvenile court to determine appropriate charges.
- No mention of marijuana was found in the police report released to media outlets WKRG, WABF, and WPMI.
- The identity of the property owner is being withheld because disclosing it would identify at least one juvenile, violating Fairhope Police policy on media relations.
- No adults were present when officers arrived; they only appeared later to collect their children. No adults were charged, and charges are unlikely due to requirements in statute 13A-11-10.1 that require an authorized adult to be present at the party.
- Fairhope Police released this information to WKRG/Channel 5, WABF/1220 AM, and WPMI/Channel 15.

AUTHOR'S ALLEGATION:
The author alleges that City Council President Jack Burrell failed to disclose a police record prior to his election and implies he was involved in the juvenile party incident at 16000 Block of County Road 13, calling for his resignation based on this alleged hidden criminal history.

